            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The second case is the observation of a large decadal change in Tropical (20 N to 20 S) outgoing longwave radiation (OLR) from the Earth Radiation Budget Satellites (ERBS) designed to measure the planetary energy balance by observing the total infrared radiation emitted by the Earth (commonly referred to as outgoing longwave radiation or OLR) and the total visible radiation (commonly referred to as SW, for \u201cshort wavelength\") reflected from the Earth.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GNSS-RO sensors",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "temperature",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The SABER calibration drift rate was determined by comparing SABER global annual mean stratospheric temperatures with the highly accurate and inherently stable temperatures derived from Global Navigation Satellite System Radio Occultation (GNSS-RO) measurements. The time delay due to refraction observed by the GNSS-RO sensors is tied to the SI definition of the second. The resulting accurate GNSS-RO temperature profiles have a trend uncertainty of less than 0.05 K/decade (Steiner et al. 2020).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "OSIRIS",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "atomic oxygen",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The O in MSIS 2.1 is largely drawn from SABER (Mlynczak et al. 2018b, 2013) and the OSIRIS instrument (Sheese et al. 2011). There will be, nevertheless, a probable dependence on the F10.7 and Ap indexes in the SABER temperatures, particularly above 90 km.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MSIS-2000",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SUBSEQUENT_VERSION",
            "target_entity_instance": "MSIS 2.0",
            "target_entity_type": "Source",
            "paragraph": "The modeled O is currently provided by the MSIS-2000 empirical model in the current version of the SABER dataset. The primary parameters driving the MSIS-2000 model are F10.7 and Ap; consequently, MSIS O \u201cbakes into\u201d the retrieved temperatures a dependence on these two indexes. This becomes problematic because F10.7 is the primary index used to account for and remove the effects of the 11-year solar cycle in trend analyses (e.g., Mlynczak et al. 2022; Garcia et al. 2019). This has an unintended effect of potentially compromising temperature trend derivations using SABER data above 87 km as the same index used in the data production is also used in the removal of natural variability in the linear regression to determine the trend. Because of the issues in the airglow-derived O, it is likely that in Version 3 of the SABER data (to be produced in 2026), the entire O profile used in the SABER temperature retrieval above 80 km will be from MSIS 2.0 (Emmert et al. 2021a).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "WACCM",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "CO2",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "To deal with this issue, SABER uses monthly mean day and night vertical profiles of CO2 provided by the Whole Atmosphere Community Climate Model (WACCM, Gettelman et al. 2019). As time goes on, data from additional WACCM runs are provided to the SABER team. In 2019, additional data were provided, but from a version of WACCM different from the one that had been used in all data processing since the mission began.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
